\section{Check the Stack at a Handler, Not at the Throw Site}

An ordinary branch preserves the operand stack left by its source
instruction. An exception transfer does not. When a JVM handler runs,
its operand stack starts with only the caught exception. The verifier
still checks the locals against the handler's frame using the state
associated with each covered instruction. The procedure below isolates
that rule for a toy method: locals have fixed verification types,
there are no uninitialized objects, and every protected instruction
has a checked frame before execution. Assume the exception table has
already been validated and each handler has a declared entry frame.
The catch type lookup returns \texttt{Throwable} for a catch-all.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Checked instruction frames $F$, handlers $H$
\ForAll{$h\in H$}
  \Let{G}{\operatorname{declaredFrame}(h)}
  \ForAll{checked $p$ in the protected range of $h$}
    \To{Keep local types from before the instruction.}
    \Let{L}{F[p].\mathrm{locals}}
    \To{Discard the normal operand stack.}
    \Let{S}{[\operatorname{catchType}(h)]}
    \If{$(L,S)$ is not assignable to $G$}
      \State \Return reject
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State \Return accept these edges
\end{sudocode}

Take an integer division with locals holding the input pair $(8,0)$.
The stack before division holds those same two integers. Protect the
division with an \texttt{ArithmeticException} handler that discards the
exception and returns $-1$. The normal successor would receive one
integer on successful division, but this invocation enters the handler
with one reference and returns $-1$. With inputs $(8,2)$ it instead
returns the normal quotient $4$. A handler frame declaring one integer
must fail the compatibility check even though its declared stack depth
is one. A useful interpreter regression checks both outcomes separately
from the verifier's rejection test.

Figure~\ref{fig:visual-198} contrasts the verifier states delivered to the two successors.

